# A ci.yml job that checks out the tree uploads its time record as a `time-record-<job>` artifact,
# or has a line here saying why it does not (milestone 808 (every gate accounts for its time), §254
# (a gate prints what each item cost)). `helpers/time_record.py lint`, run by script/lint, holds it:
#
#     <job> <reason, in words>
#
# A line with no reason is refused, and so is one for a job that uploads a record or is not in
# ci.yml. A job here still has its per-step times in notes/project-metrics/ci-job-times.csv, read
# from the jobs API, so "one command" below means that record is already the whole breakdown.
gate one shell step that classifies the diff; seconds
main-caches builds caches for other jobs and runs no gate; its per-step record covers it
rg-threads one command, a single threaded rg run per architecture; nothing inside it is an item
rg-threads-x86_64 one command, a single threaded rg run; nothing inside it is an item
boot-check two boots, each its own step, so the per-step record is the breakdown
watchdog one boot that must hang and be killed; its time is the bound by construction
fmt one rustfmt command
lint script/lint is a few hundred checks and none is timed yet; a per-check record is follow-on work in milestone 808's block
fastpath-footprint one build and one count
boot-file-size-check one build and one size check
interleavings one command, script/interleaving-check
build-is-reproducible two builds and a compare
image-permissions one build and one check
stack-frame-check two commands, each its own step
bench script/ci-build bench and icount are their own steps, and the benchmarks report counts, not wall time
supply-chain one cargo-deny command
coverage one instrumented host pass; the host pass's own time is in test-shard's record
